-  "title": "Restrictive & Explanatory Relative Clauses in Geography",
+  "id": "grammar.b2.brasilsudeste.02.relatives",
+  "title": "Rio de Janeiro: Who, Which, Where, Whose",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "La descripción del paisaje y la trama urbana carioca requiere diferenciar entre oraciones de relativo especificativas (que restringen el antecedente sin comas) y explicativas (que aportan un rasgo descriptivo entre comas)."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *que*, *quien*, *donde* and *cuyo* from B1 Unit 5: *que* for people and things, *quien* after a preposition, *donde* for places and *cuyo* for \"whose\". They let one sentence describe a place in detail, which is what a description of Rio needs: hills, bay, districts. Here they work on Rio de Janeiro."
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Oraciones de relativo en la prosa descriptiva",
+      "title": "Four relatives at work",
       "rows": [
         [
-          "Los morros graníticos que rodean la bahía crean un anfiteatro natural imponente.",
-          "The granite hills that surround the bay create an imposing natural amphitheater."
+          "Los morros que rodean la bahía forman un anfiteatro natural.",
+          "*que* for things: the hills that surround the bay"
         ],
         [
-          "El Corcovado, que se eleva a más de setecientos metros, corona la silueta de la metrópolis.",
-          "The Corcovado, which rises over seven hundred meters, crowns the silhouette of the metropolis."
+          "Los navegantes que llegaron en 1502 creyeron que la bahía era un río.",
+          "*que* for people: the sailors who arrived"
         ],
         [
-          "Las comunidades que se asientan sobre las laderas han desarrollado una rica vida cultural.",
-          "The communities that settle upon the hillsides have developed a rich cultural life."
+          "El Corcovado, donde está el Cristo Redentor, mira sobre la ciudad.",
+          "*donde* for a place: where the statue stands"
         ],
         [
-          "La bahía de Guanabara, cuyas aguas sufren contaminación histórica, alberga decenas de islas.",
-          "Guanabara Bay, whose waters suffer from historic pollution, harbors dozens of islands."
+          "La bahía de Guanabara, cuyas aguas bañan muchas playas, tiene varias islas.",
+          "*cuyas* agrees with *aguas*, the thing owned: whose waters"
         ]
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "La ciudad donde se instaló la corte portuguesa en 1808 era Río de Janeiro.",
+          "english": "The city where the Portuguese court settled in 1808 was Rio de Janeiro."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "El Pan de Azúcar, que se ve desde muchas playas, es uno de sus símbolos.",
+          "english": "Sugarloaf Mountain, which can be seen from many beaches, is one of its symbols."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Hay barrios cuyos vecinos viven en las laderas de los morros.",
+          "english": "There are districts whose residents live on the hillsides."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "El pronombre 'cuyo' concuerda en género y número con el sustantivo que le sigue inmediatamente, nunca con el poseedor antecedente: 'la bahía (fem.) cuyas aguas (fem. pl.)'."
+      "content": "Without commas, the relative clause says which one you mean (*los morros que rodean la bahía*: only those hills). Between commas, it adds extra information about something already clear (*El Corcovado, donde está el Cristo Redentor, ...*). *Cuyo* agrees with the thing owned, never with the owner, and no article comes after it."
